        <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in District 4 (East Harlem) - A Parent's Guide</h1>

  <p>District 4, encompassing East Harlem, Spanish Harlem, and Randalls Island, is committed to upholding student rights while maintaining a safe and supportive learning environment. Understanding student rights and disciplinary procedures is crucial for both students and families, especially for students with disabilities. This guide provides information and advice regarding discipline, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), and due process rights in District 4.</p>

  <h2>Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDR) - A Critical Safeguard</h2>

  <p>A Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a *critical* process mandated by the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) for students with disabilities who face suspension or expulsion. According to 34 CFR § 300.530, if a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a 504 plan is facing a suspension exceeding 10 consecutive school days, or a pattern of removals that constitute a change of placement (as defined by federal regulations), the school must conduct an MDR. The purpose of this review is to determine if the student's behavior was caused by, or directly and substantially related to, their disability, or if the behavior was the direct result of the school's failure to implement the IEP. Parents are *vital* participants in this process, and their input is crucial to a fair and accurate determination.</p>

  <p>Here's a breakdown of the MDR process:</p>

  <ol>
      <li><b>Notification:</b> The school must notify you of the disciplinary action and your rights, including the right to request an MDR. This notification must be timely.</li>
      <li><b>Review of Relevant Information:</b> The MDR team, which includes the school, relevant IEP team members (including the parent), will review all relevant information in the student's file. This includes:
          <ul>
              <li>The student's IEP (including goals, accommodations, and behavior intervention plan (BIP), if applicable)</li>
              <li>Any relevant evaluations (psychological, educational, behavioral)</li>
              <li>Teacher observations and anecdotal records</li>
              <li>Information provided by the parent(s)</li>
          </ul>
      </li>
      <li><b>Determination:</b> The team will then determine if the conduct in question was:
          <ul>
              <li>Caused by or directly and substantially related to the student's disability; *or*</li>
              <li>The direct result of the school district’s failure to implement the IEP.</li>
          </ul>
      </li>
      <li><b>Outcomes:</b>
          <ul>
              <li><b>If the behavior *was* a manifestation of the disability:</b> The IEP team must conduct a functional behavioral assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted, and implement a behavior intervention plan (BIP) for the student. If a BIP is already in place, the team must review and modify it, as necessary, to address the behavior.  The student must be returned to their placement unless the parents and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the modification of the BIP.  Note: even with a manifestation, a school can seek an interim alternative educational setting (IAES) placement for up to 45 school days if the student’s behavior involves weapons, drugs, or serious bodily injury to another person (34 CFR § 300.530(g)).</li>
              <li><b>If the behavior was *not* a manifestation of the disability:</b> The student may be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities.  However, the school must continue to provide special education services as outlined in the IEP during the period of suspension or expulsion (34 CFR § 300.530(d)).</li>
          </ul>
      </li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Document everything! Keep records of all communication with the school, including emails, letters, and phone calls. Prepare your own statement for the MDR meeting, outlining your perspective on the behavior and any factors related to your child's disability that may have contributed to it. Bring copies of any relevant medical or psychological evaluations.</p>

  <h3>Common District 4 Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them</h3>

  <ul>
      <li><b>Insufficient Notice:</b> Schools sometimes fail to provide parents with adequate notice of the MDR meeting or the disciplinary charges against their child.  According to NYS Part 200 Regulations 200.2(b)(1)(xii), parents are entitled to written notice.  <b>Your Action:</b> Immediately request a postponement of the MDR meeting if you haven't received adequate notice or sufficient information to prepare.</li>
      <li><b>Predetermined Outcomes:</b> Occasionally, the MDR outcome seems predetermined, with the school appearing unwilling to consider the parent's perspective or the student's disability. <b>Your Action:</b> Assert your right to participate meaningfully in the process. If you feel your concerns are not being heard, request that your concerns are noted in the MDR documentation. Consider bringing an advocate or attorney to the meeting.</li>
      <li><b>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</b> The behavior may be directly related to the school's failure to implement the IEP properly. <b>Your Action:</b> Prior to the MDR meeting, carefully review the IEP to ensure that all accommodations, modifications, and supports are being provided as specified.  Highlight any instances where the IEP has not been followed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Advice Regarding Suspensions in District 4</h2>

  <p>If your child in District 4 is facing suspension, it's essential to act promptly and strategically. Suspension can have significant academic and emotional consequences, especially for students with disabilities. Here’s a step-by-step guide:</p>

  <ol>
      <li><b>Understand the Charges:</b> Immediately request a meeting with school administrators (principal, assistant principal, guidance counselor) to understand the specific charges against your child and the evidence supporting those charges. Request copies of any incident reports, witness statements, or other documentation.</li>
      <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep a detailed record of all communication with the school, including dates, times, names of individuals involved, and a summary of the conversation. Save all emails and letters.</li>
      <li><b>Know Your Child's Rights:</b> Familiarize yourself with the New York City Department of Education's Discipline Code and the rights of students with disabilities under IDEA and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act.</li>
      <li><b>For Students with IEPs or 504 Plans:</b> Ensure an MDR is conducted *before* a suspension exceeding 10 days is imposed. If the school fails to initiate an MDR, demand that they do so. The MDR timeline is crucial - don't delay!</li>
      <li><b>Appeal the Decision:</b> For suspensions exceeding five school days, you have the right to appeal the decision to the Superintendent. The appeal process typically involves submitting a written statement outlining your reasons for disagreeing with the suspension. Consult with an advocate or attorney to ensure your appeal is strong and persuasive.</li>
      <li><b>Request a Superintendent's Suspension Hearing:</b> For long-term suspensions (more than 5 days), you are entitled to a hearing where you can present evidence and witnesses on your child's behalf. Make sure to request all documents the school intends to present at the hearing in advance so you can prepare a defense.</li>
      <li><b>Seek Legal Counsel:</b> If you believe your child's rights have been violated, or if you are struggling to navigate the disciplinary process, consult with a special education attorney or advocate.</li>
      <li><b>Contact District 4 Resources:</b> Reach out to the District 4 Family Support Coordinator or advocacy organizations specializing in student rights for assistance. (Contact information provided below.)</li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Emphasize your willingness to collaborate with the school to address the underlying issues contributing to your child's behavior. Suggest alternative disciplinary measures, such as restorative justice practices or counseling, that may be more effective than suspension. Request a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) and Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) if one is not already in place. </p>

  <h2>Due Process Rights in District 4</h2>

  <p>If you disagree with the school's decisions regarding your child's special education services (including disciplinary actions that are related to their disability), you have the right to pursue due process. Due process is a formal legal process that allows you to resolve disputes with the school district. It is your right under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).</p>

  <p><b>Steps to Filing a Due Process Complaint:</b></p>

  <ol>
      <li><b>Consider Mediation:</b> Before filing a formal due process complaint, consider participating in mediation with the school district. Mediation is a voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the school district reach a mutually agreeable resolution. Mediation can be a less adversarial and more efficient way to resolve disputes.</li>
      <li><b>File a Due Process Complaint:</b> To initiate due process, you must file a written complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The complaint must include specific information, such as:
          <ul>
              <li>The name and address of the child</li>
              <li>The name of the school</li>
              <li>A description of the problem, including the facts relating to the problem</li>
              <li>A proposed resolution of the problem to the extent known and available to the parent at the time</li>
          </ul>
      </li>
       <li><b>Resolution Meeting:</b> Within 15 days of receiving the due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution meeting with you and relevant members of the IEP team. The purpose of the meeting is to attempt to resolve the dispute. You can waive this meeting.</li>
      <li><b>Due Process Hearing:</b> If the dispute is not resolved through the resolution meeting, a due process hearing will be conducted by an impartial hearing officer (IHO). The hearing is similar to a court trial, where you and the school district can present evidence, call witnesses, and cross-examine witnesses.</li>
      <li><b>Hearing Officer Decision:</b> After the hearing, the IHO will issue a written decision. The decision is binding on both you and the school district, unless appealed.</li>
      <li><b>Appeal:</b> If you disagree with the IHO's decision, you can appeal to the State Review Officer (SRO).</li>
  </ol>

    <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Document everything related to your child's special education needs and any disputes with the school district. This documentation will be crucial if you pursue due process. Consult with a special education attorney or advocate to understand your rights and options.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

  <dl>
      <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between a suspension and an expulsion?</b></dt>
      <dd>A: A suspension is a temporary removal of a student from school, while an expulsion is a permanent removal. Expulsions are rare in New York City public schools, but can occur in serious cases.</dd>

      <dt><b>Q: What is an Interim Alternative Educational Setting (IAES)?</b></dt>
      <dd>A: An IAES is a temporary placement for a student with a disability who has violated the school's code of conduct. The IAES must provide the student with the opportunity to continue to participate in the general education curriculum, although in a different setting. An IAES placement can be for up to 45 school days for certain offenses (weapons, drugs, or serious bodily injury). </dd>

      <dt><b>Q: Can a student with a disability be suspended for behavior that is not related to their disability?</b></dt>
      <dd>A: Yes, but only after an MDR determines that the behavior was *not* a manifestation of the disability. Even then, the school must continue to provide special education services as outlined in the IEP during the suspension. </dd>

      <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the results of the Manifestation Determination Review?</b></dt>
      <dd>A: You have the right to challenge the MDR through due process. </dd>

      <dt><b>Q: How can I find a special education attorney or advocate in District 4?</b></dt>
      <dd>A: (List Local Resources Here, Keep Existing Links/Contacts Intact) Look for organizations that provide free or low-cost legal services to families of children with disabilities. Contact the New York State Education Department (NYSED) or disability rights organizations for referrals.</dd>

      <dt><b>Q:  My child doesn't have a formal IEP, but I suspect they have a disability. What can I do?</b></dt>
      <dd>A:  You have the right to request an evaluation from the school district to determine if your child has a disability that qualifies them for special education services.  This request should be made in writing to the school principal and the school's Committee on Special Education (CSE).</dd>

      <dt><b>Q: My child has a 504 plan, not an IEP. Do MDR rules still apply?</b></dt>
      <dd>A: Yes, MDR requirements apply to students with 504 plans facing disciplinary actions that constitute a significant change in placement.</dd>
  </dl>
    </div>

